{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"The claim is **too underspecified to judge as written**. It depends on the country, year, and how “white people” are defined in the data.\n\nIn the United States, **non-Hispanic white people have had lower life expectancy than the overall national average in some recent years**. That can happen because groups with higher life expectancy—including Hispanic and Asian populations—raise the national average. Combining Hispanic and non-Hispanic white people could yield a different comparison.\n\nSo the claim can be true for a particular population and period, but it isn’t a general fact about white people. A precise version needs a country, year, and population definition."}}
